{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 1900,
    "dDurationMs": 5800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In 1994, a massive earthquake shook the Northridge suburb of Los Angeles, killing 57"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7700,
    "dDurationMs": 7540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "people and injuring over 5000. The cost of damages was in excess of $20 billion. It's"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 15240,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earthquakes like this one that make us question just how solid is the earth beneath our"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 20760,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feet, and what does it mean to be solid anyway?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25460,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At first glance, pitch looks like a solid, but it's not. It's actually a liquid at room"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 31580,
    "dDurationMs": 7060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "temperature—just a very viscous one. Viscosity is a measure of resistance to flow—what we"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38640,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "often think of as the \"thickness\" of a liquid. Olive oil is nearly 100 times more viscous"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44480,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than water, and honey is about 100 times more viscous than that. Meanwhile, pitch has a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50460,
    "dDurationMs": 6580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viscosity 2.3×10¹¹ times that of water. At the university of Queensland in Australia,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57040,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pitch is the subject of the world's longest running lab experiment, and it's still going to this day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63760,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back in 1927, this glob of pitch was placed into a funnel, and ever since then, in nearly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70500,
    "dDurationMs": 7780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "90 years, it has produced only 9 drips—roughly 1 a decade—and no one has ever been in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 78280,
    "dDurationMs": 7760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "room to see a drop fall. Though in 1988, the former custodian of the experiment, John Mainstone,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86040,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "came very close to observing a drip fall, except he stepped out of the room"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92020,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for just a few minutes to get a cup of tea. Now, you can actually watch this experiment"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99540,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "live—there's a link in the description—but since the last drop happened in 2014, I think"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 104800,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you'll probably be waiting a while."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107240,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another substance that you may have heard is a very viscous liquid is glass. If you look"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111780,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at the stained glass windows of old churches, you'll find the bottom of the pane is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116080,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "decidedly thicker than the top, and that's because the glass has been flowing down over"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 121020,
    "dDurationMs": 7480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "centuries... Actually, no, it hasn't! You know, we've looked at old telescopes where the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128500,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "optics is very sensitive to slight shifts in the lens glass, and we find they still work"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133520,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perfectly after hundreds of years. Plus, studies of thousand year old windows find no"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139160,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "real evidence of flow. So the truth is that it's just very difficult to make glass of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 144880,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "uniform thickness, and so when the glass was originally installed thousands of years ago,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150080,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they would install the thickest part towards the bottom. The lead actually has a lower"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 154780,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viscosity than the glass, so if the glass had even thickened a little bit, then the lead"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159680,
    "dDurationMs": 2820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "should be a puddle on the floor by now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162500,
    "dDurationMs": 5920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, glass is unusual in that it's an amorphous solid, meaning that the silica molecules"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168420,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "are not regularly arranged as in a regular crystalline lattice. Instead, they're all in a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174420,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "jumble, and this is because the glass is cooled down so quickly from the liquid state to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 179400,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solid state that the molecules don't have time to arrange themselves in a nice"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183760,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "regular crystal structure—but what makes something a solid rather than a liquid is that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188820,
    "dDurationMs": 5240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of the atoms or molecules are so strongly bonded together chemically that they can't"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194060,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "slide past each other. So in water, or olive oil, or pitch, the molecules can slide past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 200380,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "each other, but in glass, at room temperature, they can't."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 204140,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what about the interior of the Earth? Beneath the Earth's crust is the mantle, which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209740,
    "dDurationMs": 6760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is responsible for plate tectonics, and therefore earthquakes. Is it a solid or a liquid?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 216500,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can obviously never observe the mantle directly, but when we do see material come out"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221780,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from underground, it is red hot rock—it's lava. So you might be imagining that the mantle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 228260,
    "dDurationMs": 6480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is very similar, made up of this molten magma—hot liquid rock—and that would make sense,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 234740,
    "dDurationMs": 6380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "because in order for it to flow, it must be a liquid, right? Actually, wrong! The mantle"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241120,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a solid. Under all that pressure, even though it's at very high temperature, it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245900,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remains in a solid state, and we know the mantle is solid because shear waves from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 251400,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "earthquakes can actually propagate through the mantle. These waves cannot propagate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 255680,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "through liquid, like the molten iron of Earth's outer core, because liquids flow in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260900,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "response to being sheared—or rubbed sideways—and as a result, we can see the shadow of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 266620,
    "dDurationMs": 4820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the liquid outer core by measuring seismic waves from an earthquake on the other side of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271440,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the world—but how exactly does this solid rock flow?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 275400,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, the answer lies in the fact that crystals aren't perfect. There may be a missing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280060,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom here or there, and under the high pressures in the mantle, sometimes a neighboring"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 284860,
    "dDurationMs": 5260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "atom will pop in to fill that gap. Now, from a human perspective it takes a very long"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290120,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time for this to have a noticeable effect, but from the Earth's perspective, it happens"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294780,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in no time at all. The viscosity of the mantle is similar to that of glass, to several"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 301120,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "orders of magnitude greater, so it is really only over these geological time scales that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306860,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the mantle is fluid-like at all."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 310240,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So pitch—a liquid—can flow so slowly as to seem like a solid, whereas the Earth's mantle—"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 316500,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a solid—behaves like a fluid if you just wait long enough. As the famous American"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322480,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "geologist Grove Karl Gilbert once said:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325360,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "\"To my mind it appears that the difficulty is only imaginary and not real. Rigidity and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 330320,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "plasticity are not absolute terms, but relative, and all solids are in fact both rigid"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335820,
    "dDurationMs": 6080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and plastic... When great masses and great forces are involved ... the distinction loses value.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 341900,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Sometimes the rigid definitions we create for ourselves can introduce misconceptions, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348160,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "viscous rumors, like the idea that the core of the Earth is a giant ball of magma. If"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354680,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only we could think about liquids and solids a little bit more...fluidly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367040,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hey! This episode of Veritasium was supported by Audible.com, a leading provider of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371460,
    "dDurationMs": 4520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "audiobooks, with over 180,000 titles in all areas of literature, including fiction,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 375980,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "non-fiction and periodicals. This week I wanted to recommend to you a brand new"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 380400,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "science-fiction book published in 2015. It is by Neal Stephenson, and the book is called"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384960,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "'Seveneves', and it's about a massive catastrophe that threatens the Earth. I think that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390480,
    "dDurationMs": 3700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if you're into science, you will really enjoy it, and you can actually download this book"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394180,
    "dDurationMs": 3520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for free by going to audible.com/veritasium, or you can pick any other book of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 397700,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "your choosing for a one month free trial."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399920,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So, I really wanna thank Audible for supporting me and I wanna thank you for watching,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404320,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and if you haven't signed up for my mailing list yet, that is something you should"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 407880,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "probably do. You can click on this card here and go over to Veritasium.com, and that is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 412720,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where I will be announcing some very cool things coming out very soon. So yeah,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418080,
    "dDurationMs": 2180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "check it out, if you haven't already. Bye!"
    } ]
  } ]
}
